1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,072
Water network sensors can reveal a problem without showing exactly where it is.

2
00:00:05,072 --> 00:00:13,377
Leak Probe explores a practical response: compare possible explanations,
identify the most useful measurement, and update the result.

3
00:00:13,377 --> 00:00:16,998
This five minute walkthrough uses the actual local demo output.

4
00:00:16,998 --> 00:00:20,872
Every reading is synthetic, and the investigation sequence is scripted.

5
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:28,919
The example has one water supply and two matching branches.

6
00:00:28,919 --> 00:00:32,445
A flow sensor and a pressure sensor sit upstream of the split.

7
00:00:32,445 --> 00:00:34,195
Either branch can develop a leak.

8
00:00:34,195 --> 00:00:40,503
Because the branches are symmetric, these upstream sensors produce the same
modeled response for leak A and leak B.

9
00:00:40,503 --> 00:00:43,131
More upstream readings cannot resolve that tie.

10
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:57,253
The demo uses W N T R to simulate normal operation, a leak on branch A, and a
leak on branch B.

11
00:00:57,253 --> 00:01:02,248
Supply head is forty two meters, and each end junction requests five liters per
second.

12
00:01:02,248 --> 00:01:06,099
A sixteen millimeter leak opens halfway along one branch at two twenty.

13
00:01:06,099 --> 00:01:08,944
The simulator computes the resulting flow and pressure.

14
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:18,596
The local walkthrough runs with Python three point twelve.

15
00:01:18,596 --> 00:01:24,422
Create a virtual environment, activate it, install the requirements, and run the
demo script.

16
00:01:24,422 --> 00:01:30,156
That command generates the prediction files and observations, then executes the
investigation tools.

17
00:01:30,156 --> 00:01:32,000
This run completed successfully.

18
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:37,906
It uses the local simulator, so the walkthrough needs no model credentials or
paid inference requests.

19
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:46,647
The observation file contains ten readings at five minute intervals, from two o
clock to two forty five.

20
00:01:46,647 --> 00:01:52,987
The synthetic upstream flow rises from about ten to fourteen point one five
liters per second after the leak begins.

21
00:01:52,987 --> 00:01:54,680
Pressure also falls slightly.

22
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:57,631
Small reproducible noise is added to observations.

23
00:01:57,631 --> 00:02:01,658
These signals support a leak scenario, but leave its branch unresolved.

24
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:11,220
The comparison tool aligns the timestamps and scores the flow and pressure
residuals against each scenario.

25
00:02:11,220 --> 00:02:16,089
Before a branch measurement, both leak scenarios score zero point one two three
seven.

26
00:02:16,089 --> 00:02:19,532
Normal operation scores eleven point four two seven zero.

27
00:02:19,532 --> 00:02:21,765
Lower normalized error means a better fit.

28
00:02:21,765 --> 00:02:27,731
These scores are neither probabilities nor confidence levels, and only three
fixed explanations are compared.

29
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:33,607
The tools also expose fictional maintenance records.

30
00:02:33,607 --> 00:02:36,059
One record schedules flushing on branch B.

31
00:02:36,059 --> 00:02:40,003
A later linked record says that flushing was cancelled and the crew reassigned.

32
00:02:40,003 --> 00:02:43,287
That context matters when interpreting an apparent leak signal.

33
00:02:43,287 --> 00:02:51,968
However, this starter simulator does not model flushing or sensor bias, and the
numeric ranking does not automatically interpret the maintenance notes.

34
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:59,676
The planner compares the predicted branch pressures for the two leak scenarios.

35
00:02:59,676 --> 00:03:03,767
Both measurement points offer about one point six three meters of separation.

36
00:03:03,767 --> 00:03:07,463
But visiting M A takes fifteen minutes, while M B takes thirty.

37
00:03:07,463 --> 00:03:10,484
With twenty minutes available, only M A is feasible.

38
00:03:10,484 --> 00:03:15,198
The tool chooses pressure separation per minute using a simple demonstration
heuristic.

39
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:26,626
The collection tool reveals the synthetic M A reading: thirty seven point eight
zero nine meters of pressure head.

40
00:03:26,626 --> 00:03:31,216
Leak A predicts about thirty seven point seven seven nine meters at that
location.

41
00:03:31,216 --> 00:03:33,926
Leak B predicts thirty nine point four one one.

42
00:03:33,926 --> 00:03:36,806
The visit consumes fifteen minutes, leaving five.

43
00:03:36,806 --> 00:03:41,727
This fixture assumes the final simulated hydraulic state stays steady until
collection.

44
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:49,492
With the branch reading included, the tool compares the scenarios again.

45
00:03:49,492 --> 00:03:55,440
Leak A now scores zero point one two five one, while leak B scores one point
seven five two two.

46
00:03:55,440 --> 00:03:59,498
Normal operation remains a poor fit at eleven point three seven nine nine.

47
00:03:59,498 --> 00:04:03,520
Branch A best explains this synthetic evidence among the modeled candidates.

48
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:06,049
That result does not confirm a real field leak.

49
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:13,677
The full tool sequence is saved in the scripted trace file.

50
00:04:13,677 --> 00:04:20,686
It includes the observations, network, maintenance notes, rankings,
recommendation, and collected reading.

51
00:04:20,686 --> 00:04:24,191
Prediction files expose the underlying simulation results.

52
00:04:24,191 --> 00:04:34,650
This evidence trail is reviewable: you can see exactly why the result changes
when a branch reading distinguishes two explanations that previously matched the
upstream sensors equally well.

53
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:39,900
Leak Probe demonstrates how a limited measurement budget can guide an
investigation.

54
00:04:39,900 --> 00:04:44,753
The next extension is an optional language model that chooses tools through the
same interface.

55
00:04:44,753 --> 00:04:47,236
That live path is separate and was not used here.

56
00:04:47,236 --> 00:04:55,716
Before field use, the system would need calibrated uncertainty, alternative
scenarios, and validation against independent observations.

57
00:04:55,716 --> 00:04:59,071
Today, this is a reproducible educational prototype.
